WEBVTT

i0.1
00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:06.050
This video shows the semaphore of doeff, which limits how many tasks run at once.

i0.2
00:00:06.050 --> 00:00:09.700
doeff is a library for algebraic effects in Python.

i0.3
00:00:09.700 --> 00:00:16.800
We follow one function, named call, which takes a permit, does its work, and gives the permit back.

i1
00:00:17.350 --> 00:00:21.850
A semaphore is a fixed number of permits that tasks take turns holding.

i2.1
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:28.017
Create Semaphore, Acquire Semaphore and Release Semaphore are effects.

i2.2
00:00:28.017 --> 00:00:31.550
The scheduler answers them, like Spawn and Wait.

i3.1
00:00:32.350 --> 00:00:39.233
A task that finds no permit is parked: the scheduler holds its k, and the other tasks run on.

i3.2
00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:40.500
No thread blocks.

p1
00:00:41.350 --> 00:00:48.850
Create Semaphore of two: four calls share two permits, so at most two are inside at once.

p2
00:00:49.850 --> 00:00:57.100
C and D find no permit. Their k's wait in the semaphore's queue, in the order they asked.

p3
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:05.700
Each release lets the next one in: C at one second, D at two. All four finish by four seconds.

f1
00:01:07.350 --> 00:01:12.750
With one permit, a semaphore is a mutex. Waiters are served first come, first served.

f2
00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:21.300
A release hands the permit straight to the oldest waiter. It never sits free for another task to grab.

f3
00:01:21.850 --> 00:01:26.900
So late, which asks right after the release, queues behind second and third.

c1
00:01:30.350 --> 00:01:35.650
Cancel a task while it waits for a permit, and the scheduler takes it out of the queue.

c2
00:01:36.850 --> 00:01:42.850
w1 resumes with a Task Cancelled Error at its Acquire. It never took a permit.

c3
00:01:43.400 --> 00:01:49.000
So when the holder releases at two seconds, the permit goes to w2.

l1
00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:58.200
A semaphore does not track who holds a permit. Fail before Release Semaphore, and that permit is lost.

l2
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:07.500
Without try and finally, the next task is still waiting five seconds later, and would wait forever.

l3
00:02:08.050 --> 00:02:14.400
With try and finally, the release runs on failure too, and the next task enters at once.

o1.1
00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:23.833
doeff: permits are effects, and a waiting task is a parked k that the scheduler resumes in order.

o1.2
00:02:23.833 --> 00:02:30.300
Another handler can answer the same effects, say, across a cluster. pip install doeff.
